<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  DEBUG BUILDS ONLY — this file lives in src/debug/, so it is not compiled
  into a release APK or AAB at all, and neither is the manifest attribute
  that points at it (src/debug/AndroidManifest.xml). A release build has no
  cleartext permission of any kind.

  Why the whole of cleartext and not a host list:

  The dev flavor talks to a Next.js server on the developer's machine. An
  EMULATOR reaches it at 10.0.2.2 and a tunnelled device at localhost, and
  both were listed here — but a PHYSICAL phone reaches it at the Mac's LAN
  address, which changes with the network, the router's lease and whether
  anyone is on a hotspot. Pinning that address means the config is wrong
  again the next time someone moves desk, and the failure it produces is a
  silent connection error that looks like the server being down.

  So debug permits cleartext and release permits none. That is the honest
  split: the risk of cleartext is what an attacker on the same network can
  read, and a debug build pointed at a laptop has nothing in it that is not
  already on that laptop.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />
</network-security-config>
